    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but it feels a little too \"standard corporate.\" Could you make it sound a bit more warm and personable? Like, maybe something that feels less robotic and more like there are real humans behind the decision, rather than just a generic HR department. I want them to feel like we actually appreciated them.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right; the previous draft felt a bit too stiff, like a letter written by a committee of invisible bureaucrats. To make it feel warm and human, we need to inject some genuine emotion and perhaps a touch of humor about the \"final round\" struggle.\n\nHere is a revised version that feels much more personal, acknowledging the candidate's hard work while explaining the decision with empathy.\n\n***\n\n**Subject:** A personal note regarding your interview for [Job Title]\n\nHi [Candidate Name],\n\nFirst off, thank you for the incredible energy and great conversation you brought to our final round. We genuinely enjoyed getting to know you, and honestly, it was a pleasure having you in the room.\n\nWe want to be completely transparent: this wasn't an easy decision to make. We were so impressed by your skills that we actually had a few internal debates about whether you should be the one we hired. However, after weighing the specific nuances of our current team's immediate needs against your unique background, we've decided to move forward with another candidate who, at this very specific moment, fits our immediate roadmap a bit more perfectly.\n\nPlease don't take this as a reflection on your talent or your potential. You are clearly a",
      "source": "target",
